                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: 'Running' and 'Selling' Are Two Completely Different Capabilities</h2>
<p>Many performance marketing teams fall into a mental trap during creative reviews: <strong>using 'runnable' metrics to judge creative success</strong>. A 3% CTR, $12 CPM, and $1,000 daily spend looks promising—but ROI turns out to be double the target. </p>
<p>The core issue: <strong>'Running' measures your creative's ability to capture attention; 'Selling' measures its ability to drive purchase action</strong>. These two are correlated, but far less than you might expect.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Dimensions Separating 'Runnable' from 'Sellable'</h2>
<h3>1. User Intent Matching</h3>
<p>Runnable creatives excel at <strong>creating novelty or conflict in the first 3 seconds</strong>, making users stop and watch. Comedy skits, counter-intuitive visuals, and exaggerated expressions are common tactics. Such creatives won't have poor CTRs, but users are in "entertainment mode," not "problem-solving mode."</p>
<p>Sellable creatives carry <strong>clear purchase intent signals from the start</strong>. When users see the ad, they already understand why they might need this product. Data from a utility app confirms this: purely entertainment-oriented creatives had 40% higher CTR than feature-focused ones, but the latter delivered registered users with 55% lower CAC.</p>
<p><strong>Review action</strong>: Check 'Ad Recall' metrics in your dashboard. If users can't recall the product name after viewing, even the most viral creative is generating brand exposure for your competitors.</p>
<h3>2. Landing Page Synergy</h3>
<p>Runnable creatives often <strong>stand alone</strong>, generating decent clicks without requiring landing page support. But once users click through and find a completely different narrative—mismatched color tones, inconsistent value propositions, tonal dissonance—the conversion chain breaks.</p>
<p>Sellable creatives and landing pages are <strong>symbiotic</strong>. From hook to trust builder to call-to-action, the full loop must close. For example, if the creative's main hook is "saves time," the landing page's hero section needs corresponding data or scenario imagery immediately. A cross-border e-commerce team ran A/B tests: swapping the landing page hero from a model pose to a usage scenario image boosted conversion rate 2.3x with the same creative.</p>
<p><strong>Review action</strong>: Don't review only ad platform data. Pull landing page bounce rates and add-to-cart rates, then cross-analyze with creative performance. If you have high clicks but high bounce rates, the problem is rarely the creative—it's in the 'promise-fulfillment' handoff.</p>
<h3>3. User Decision Stage Alignment</h3>
<p>Runnable creatives excel at forcibly capturing attention from users <strong>completely unprepared</strong>. This is the top-of-funnel action. Sellable creatives must answer: <strong>what decision stage is this user currently in?</strong></p>
<p>High-ticket products don't suit impulse-driven 'runnable' creatives. User purchase decisions may require 7-14 days. The creative's job isn't "convert immediately" but "leave a deep enough impression for users to return when ready." A home goods brand once paused their volume-driving "low-price promotion" creatives and switched to "lifestyle" creatives. ROI began outperformance on day 30, because they accumulated users 'seeded' but not yet converted, who completed purchases through branded search later.</p>
<p><strong>Review action</strong>: Set appropriate attribution windows. Don't judge all creatives by T+1 ROI. For products requiring longer decision cycles, examine T+7 or T+14 data minimum.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Practical Checklist: Four Questions to Answer During Review</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Can users name 'what problem this product solves' after viewing the creative?</strong> If not, even the most clickable creative is money wasted.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Does the landing page immediately support the creative's main selling point?</strong> Compare screenshots of both the creative and landing page—check for tonal consistency.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>For this creative's target users, were they 'pulled' in or 'led' in?</strong> The latter (led by needs) typically delivers more stable conversion rates.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Does your attribution window match the product's decision cycle?</strong> First determine how many days of data to examine, then judge whether the creative is truly 'sellable.'</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Creative 'runnability' is baseline competence; 'sellability' is true skill. During reviews, examine 'CTR' and 'conversion rate' separately, and 'spend' and 'ROI' separately. You'll discover some creatives are natural traffic generators while others are natural conversion engines—<strong>understanding each creative's role matters more than pursuing an all-in-one performer</strong>.</p>
